WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:07.740
Vsauce!

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:13.519
Kevin here, in the control room of the USS
Pasadena -- a Los Angeles Class nuclear-powered

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:15.410
fast attack submarine.

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:21.040
The Pasadena is over 100 meters long, has
a crew of nearly 130, and is propelled by

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.830
a thermal-neutron reactor that generates 35,000
shaft horsepower.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:32.300
And it has a lot of buttons, so I’m gonna
push… this one.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.300
Hey.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.620
Don’t touch that.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.080
Or… anything.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.080
Right.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:42.790
I’m here with Petty Officer John Davis,
Machinist Mate Nuclear First Class and trained

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.390
rescue diver with the United States Navy.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:50.851
I’m 100 meters deep in the Pacific Ocean
with an expert in thermodynamics, nuclear

00:00:50.851 --> 00:00:54.280
reactor technology, fluid dynamics, mathematics
and more.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.489
And we’re gonna tap that knowledge to get
to the bottom of a game that’s been a staple

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:03.239
of kitchen tables for a century: Battleship.

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:07.409
Battleship evolved from a pencil and paper
French game called L’Attaque, which eventually

00:01:07.409 --> 00:01:08.650
became Stratego.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:13.729
By the 1960s Milton Bradley developed the
classic 10 x 10 grid in which each player

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:20.590
places 5 ships: a carrier that occupies 5
spaces, a battleship on 4, and a cruiser and

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:25.189
submarine that are each on 3, and a destroyer
on 2.

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:28.329
Ships can’t overlap, but they can touch.

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:33.470
The actual gameplay is really simple: the
first player guesses a location on the grid

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:39.479
-- say, C4 -- and the second player says whether
that shot hit one of their ships or missed

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:40.479
entirely.

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:44.680
If it’s a miss, then we both mark our grids
with a white peg.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.979
If it’s a hit, then we'll mark it with a
red one.

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:52.700
The object is to hit each location on all
5 ships and sink them.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.090
And it seems like there’s really no perfect
way to start.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:00.829
We’ve got 100 possibilities, and all of
them equally valid.

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:08.130
With 17 possible hits, our first random shot
is going to hit about 1 in 5 times.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.130
So…

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:10.530
I’ll start.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.530
F7.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:12.530
Miss.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:16.510
Random guesses in Battleship are just a massive
exercise in probability, and a simulation

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:21.330
by Nick Berry of DataGenetics showed that
using this random system means over 99% of

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.140
games will require at least 78 shots.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.460
Uhh.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.770
E7.

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:29.080
Miss.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.720
Playing totally randomly would be really,
really inefficient, and unless both players

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.790
are choosing all shots randomly, you’d lose
almost every time.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:42.140
And real randomness is actually pretty, pretty
hard.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.580
Research as early as the 1930s showed that
humans aren’t capable of generating a random

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:52.740
number sequence, and modern research in economics
shows that decision-making is incredibly difficult

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.870
when we have to try to make sense of randomness.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.010
A3.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.160
Miss.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.220
Computers are a lot better at handling randomness.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.580
But pure randomness just isn’t optimal here.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:08.100
The way most people play Battleship is a blend…
first, they shoot randomly until they get

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:14.420
a hit, and then they go up, down, left and
right around that hit to find the next part

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.510
of the ship.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:17.510
D5.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:18.510
Ugh.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:19.510
Hit!

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:23.180
That’s what Berry calls the “Hunt and
Target” method.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:29.200
You start by randomly firing shots, and then
work around your hits to sink ships.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.750
By repeating that strategy, the average game
will take about 65 shots.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:38.380
That’s better than random, but it’s not
great.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:39.950
H8.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.510
Miss.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:45.500
The next level strategy is what’s called
“Parity,” where you recognize that the

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.690
board of 100 spaces is actually no more than
50.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:52.951
Because the smallest ship has to occupy at
least 2 spaces, we can think of the Battleship

00:03:52.951 --> 00:03:55.730
board like a checkerboard of alternating colors.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:04:00.150
We can “Hunt” on just one color, and then
“Target” when we get a hit.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.280
D6.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.780
Hit… and sunk.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:10.270
Battleship’s smallest unit is 2, so once
the Destroyer has been sunk -- like you just

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:15.320
did -- then that expands to 3, then 4 and
5.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:20.400
Hunt and Target with Parity improves our average
number of moves nearer to 60, so it’s a

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.620
little better.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.940
But still not optimal.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.249
Uhh.

00:04:25.249 --> 00:04:26.560
B2.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:27.870
Miss.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:32.490
We can employ a rough algorithm in our minds
that takes into account both the board and

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:37.650
whether ships of a certain length are likely
to be in a certain position on that board.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:38.930
G9.

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:40.210
Uhh.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:41.490
Miss!

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:45.690
A computer can do this perfectly judging the
probability of a given ship’s length being

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.900
placed in the leftover spaces on the board.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:54.259
This will change with every single move and
will create a sort of heat map that suggests

00:04:54.259 --> 00:04:56.520
where we should guess next.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.430
I'm gonna guess.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:04:59.430
next...

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:00.430
D4.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:01.430
Hit!

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.960
You’ll start the game with no information
at all, so the best option is to shoot near

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.139
the center and adjust from there.

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:12.090
Guess, recalibrate, repeat, each time refining
your probability based on the layout of spaces

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:16.150
left and the length of the ships you haven’t
sunk.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:17.150
A9.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:18.150
Hit!

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:23.199
We can’t do this as fast or as perfectly
as a computer, but we can generate a pretty

00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:26.020
good approximation in our minds.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:31.100
As we play each game of Battleship, we’re
employing a probability density function and

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:35.190
refining an algorithm in our brains with every
move.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:40.430
Statistically, there’s a massive payoff
to this approach: It drops the number of shots

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:46.919
required into the 30s or 40s, with Berry finding
that all 100 million games he simulated were

00:05:46.919 --> 00:05:50.749
completed by about 65 moves.

00:05:50.749 --> 00:05:51.969
C4.

00:05:51.969 --> 00:05:53.189
Hit.

00:05:53.189 --> 00:05:59.030
That’s how so much of practical math, science
and engineering works.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:03.490
We make an approximation, and then hone in
on the details the best we can considering

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:07.680
all the variables, unknowns, and randomness
-- and then put it together with constant

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.810
adjustments to make something complex function
flawlessly.

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:11.849
B9.

00:06:11.849 --> 00:06:12.900
Ugh.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:13.940
Hit.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:16.990
It’s a constant process.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:23.029
But whether you're optimizing the probability
behind a 50-year-old board game or navigating

00:06:23.029 --> 00:06:30.150
one of the most advanced naval vessels ever
created... today we have an opportunity to

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:35.550
learn, refine, and evolve faster than ever
before.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:36.849
Wait.

00:06:36.849 --> 00:06:42.069
Did you say, B4?

00:06:42.069 --> 00:06:44.680
Uhhh.. yeah?

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:47.430
Hit… and you sunk my submarine.

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:48.430
W..wait.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:49.430
Not...

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.120
Not this submarine?

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:52.350
... No.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:53.580
Okay.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:54.810
Good.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:59.550
And as always -- thanks for watching!

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:03.689
To check out more videos like this go to sailorvs.com.

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:05.930
Did I say it right?

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:08.169
I said it right, right?

00:07:08.169 --> 00:07:09.169
Okay cool.

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:10.169
Phew!

00:07:10.169 --> 00:07:12.159
And it's got a lot of buttons.

00:07:12.159 --> 00:07:14.729
So I'm gonna touch this one.

00:07:14.729 --> 00:07:17.770
Please don't touch that!

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:18.770
Uh.

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:19.770
Right.

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:20.810
Or anything.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:21.810
Sorry.

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:22.810
Nuclear.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:25.379
I'm such an idiot.

00:07:25.379 --> 00:07:26.379
Okay yeah!

00:07:26.379 --> 00:07:29.050
If you like it -- I like it!

